# requires: arch=arm64 -threaded

# Protected-VM (pKVM) host->hyp lifecycle, pvmfw-less (Strategy 1).
# Create a protected VM (the helper forces KVM_VM_TYPE_ARM_PROTECTED / bit 31) -> create and
# KVM_ARM_VCPU_INIT a vCPU -> KVM_RUN. The hyp VM object (pkvm_create_hyp_vm) is built on the
# vCPU's *first* KVM_RUN (arch/arm64/kvm/arm.c:755-759, kvm_arch_vcpu_run_pid_change), NOT at
# VCPU_INIT.
# CAVEAT (verified on N90, -threaded=0): pvmfw-less, KVM_RUN does NOT return promptly -- the vCPU
# enters at the host-set reset PC and BLOCKS, so the executor's timeout kills the worker at
# KVM_RUN and the two closes below do NOT execute (completed=4). Teardown (pkvm_destroy_hyp_vm)
# still fires -- from kernel fd-cleanup when the killed worker's VM fd drops. When the harness
# DOES reach the closes (e.g. threaded, or KVM_RUN made to exit), teardown comes from them
# instead: the vCPU fd is closed before the VM (vCPU fds hold a VM ref, so the VM close is the
# last reference). Both paths reach the same pkvm_destroy_hyp_vm. No guest payload/memory, no
# SET_FW_IPA. N90 evidence: kretprobe pkvm_create_hyp_vm $retval=0; KCOV on pkvm.c
# pkvm_init_host_vm. docs/superpowers/specs/evidence/2026-07-21-phaseB-n90-reverification.md

r0 = openat$kvm(0xffffffffffffff9c, &AUTO='/dev/kvm\x00', 0x2, 0x0)
r1 = syz_kvm_setup_protected_vm$arm64(r0, 0x0)
r2 = ioctl$KVM_CREATE_VCPU(r1, AUTO, 0x0)
ioctl$KVM_ARM_VCPU_INIT(r2, AUTO, &AUTO={0x5, 0x0, ""})
ioctl$KVM_RUN(r2, AUTO, 0x0)
close(r2)
close$kvmvm_protected(r1)
